text:
The user wants to combine the two selected widgets:
1. `fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder` - finds the closest sticky note and shows its text and distance
2. `fluid.demos.imageGenerator` - generates an image from a prompt

The combined widget should: let the user move it close to a sticky note and press a button to generate an image. So the image prompt should come from the closest note's text.

Design: Create a new umbrella/fresh layer that combines both. The strategy hints suggest:
- Define a fresh layer which lists existing layers in `$layers`, followed by `fluid.topLevelWidget`, holding only overrides.
- Or compose two widgets as subcomponents of an umbrella layer.

But wait — combining these two is tricky. The closestNoteFinder's `compile` for prompt: We want the "Find Image" button (or a new button) to use the closest note's text as the prompt. The imageGenerator currently uses `state.prompt` (typed). We want the prompt to come from the closest note.

Approaches:

Option A: Umbrella layer with both as subcomponents, with the imageGenerator's prompt overridden to `closestNoteFinder.closest.text`. But then the imageGenerator's text input would show/write a different value — if we override `state.prompt` with a reference, the user can't type. We might want to keep the text input too? The user says "press a button to generate an image" — the prompt comes from the closest sticky note. We could use the distance line area or add a button "Generate image from closest note".

Option B: A fresh layer that merges both widget layers: `$layers: ["fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder", "fluid.demos.imageGenerator", "fluid.topLevelWidget"]` — but both have `view` subcomponents and both define `state` etc. The imageGenerator defines `state` with `$reactiveRoot` — merging would conflict; closestNoteFinder doesn't have `state`, so the merged widget would have imageGenerator's state plus closestNoteFinder's closest/noteText/distance. The view subcomponents would merge? Both define `view` as `$component` records — merging `view.$component` — view subcomponent is created per record... Actually the `view` of the merged widget: each layer contributes `view: {$component: {...}}`. The `subcomponent` precedence record would merge the bodies. closestNoteFinder's view layer isn't specified explicitly — it comes from the SFC template (fluid.sfcTemplateViewComponent). Hmm, actually in the closestNoteFinder SFC, there's no explicit view in the script, so the SFC loader adds one. When we define a new layer as an SFC, the loader adds its own view with the new template. As a plain JS layer, we'd need to define view ourselves.

Wait — but if the fresh layer is defined as an SFC with a `<template>`, the loader defaults `$layers` to `["fluid.component", "fluid.sfcLayer"]`... Actually the SFC loader adds a view with the template from the SFC. But for a widget we also need `fluid.topLevelWidget` — the instruction says every synthesized widget's top-level layer must name `fluid.topLevelWidget` explicitly as the last of its layers.

If the layer is an SFC, the loader sets `$layers` default to `["fluid.component", "fluid.sfcLayer"]` but our script can define `$layers: [...]` explicitly. We must include `fluid.topLevelWidget` as the last entry.

Hmm, but the view: the SFC loader adds the view subcomponent with the template. If the layer also inherits views from parent layers (closestNoteFinder, imageGenerator), the view records would merge. The templateViewComponent layer from SFC sets `template` — a strong precedence would override the template. Actually the merging: `view.$component` body merges at subcomponent precedence. Parent layers (weakest) ... the SFC-added view record (`$layers: "fluid.sfcTemplateViewComponent"`) — with what precedence? The loader "adds" it. Probably at some precedence comparable to... The docs say "A view inherited from another layer accumulates the configuration, by the usual merging rules." So the view records from parent layers merge, and the SFC's template would be the strongest, overriding templates from others? But you can't have two templates. If closestNoteFinder and imageGenerator each add `view: {$component: {...}}` — wait, closestNoteFinder's script doesn't define view at all; its view comes purely from the SFC loader. In our new SFC, our script defines `$layers` including both widgets — but the loader adds view with `fluid.sfcTemplateViewComponent`. The inherited layers don't define `view` in their script... so no conflict: the merged view is just the SFC template view. 

But wait — imageGenerator's script DOES define subcomponents: errorMessage, loadingMessage, result, keyDown, generate, showAnother, refresh, state, isGenerating, error, image, cannotGenerate, cannotShowAnother, buttonLabel. And closestNoteFinder defines closest, noteText, distance, distanceLine, preferredSize.

Merging the layers: The `view` of imageGenerator is defined... in the script? No — imageGenerator script doesn't define `view` either; it's an SFC so the loader adds the view. Its template has `@id="errorMessage"`, `@id="loadingMessage"`, `@id="result"` etc.

So if our new SFC's template provides `@id` elements for `distanceLine`, `errorMessage`, `loadingMessage`, `result`, plus the input/buttons — we'd basically recreate a combined template. That's copying a lot.

Alternative cleaner approach: umbrella composition with two subcomponents as the strategy hint describes, and give the umbrella its own template with `@id` elements for each subcomponent. But then the imageGenerator subcomponent renders into our `@id` element using its own SFC template (fluid.selfTemplateViewComponent). The umbrella's template holds `@id="finder"` and `@id="generator"` elements. Cross-injection: override generator's prompt: `state: {prompt: "{finder}.noteText"}`. Hmm — overriding `state` with a partial object — state is `{$reactiveRoot: {...}}` in imageGenerator's layer; our umbrella's `state: {prompt: "{finder}.noteText"}` record would merge with it? The umbrella's configuration provides `state` at... what precedence? In the umbrella layer, `generator: {$component: {$layers: "fluid.demos.imageGenerator", state: {prompt: "{finder}.noteText"}}}` — this is a `subcomponent` precedence record - the strongest after live. It would merge with imageGenerator's `state` record. But `state` is a `{$reactiveRoot: ...}` signal record — "A key holding a signal record is replaced as a unit by a stronger layer's value at that key; the record is not merged into." Hmm, that means our `{prompt: ...}` wouldn't merge inside the reactiveRoot; it would replace the whole state! That would break `specific` — the imageGenerator's methods reference `state.images` etc.

Wait — but the strategy hint explicitly says: "A reference may equally override a single member of a widget's reactive state, e.g. `state: {prompt: "{first}.text"}`: the member then takes the referenced value whenever that changes, and may still be written as before." So evidently merging into a $reactiveRoot object works for plain members — the $reactiveRoot record's value is merged. Hmm, the merging rules say signal records are replaced as a unit... but the hint says otherwise for $reactiveRoot members. Perhaps because $reactiveRoot is processed specially — a stronger record updates the reactive root immutably. The hint is authoritative here: it says overriding a single member of reactive state works. OK.

So umbrella approach:

```js
fluid.def("fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator", {
    $layers: "fluid.topLevelWidget",
    preferredSize: {w: 360, h: 600} (or combine),
    finder: {
        $component: {
            $layers: "fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder"
        }
    },
    generator: {
        $component: {
            $layers: "fluid.demos.imageGenerator",
            state: {prompt: "{finder}.noteText"}
        }
    },
    view: {
        $component: {
            template: `<div class="note-imagegen">
                <div @id="finder"></div>
                <div @id="generator"></div>
            </div>`
        }
    }
});
```

Wait — but the widgets each have a `fluid.topLevelWidget` base which presumably includes their view wiring with tldraw (shapeId etc.). When used as subcomponents, their view comes from their own SFC templates via fluid.selfTemplateViewComponent (markup placed in @id element). The subcomponent's `view` — imageGenerator's layer sets view via SFC loader with `fluid.sfcTemplateViewComponent`, which uses "The `<template>` block of the SFC named by `templateLayer`". Hmm — if we instantiate it as a subcomponent from our JS layer (not loading its SFC), how does its view get the template? The tldraw integration presumably loads the SFC layer registration — actually when a component is instantiated from layer fluid.demos.imageGenerator, the framework loads the layer definition (via $importMap presumably registered by the demo app) — the SFC loader runs and adds view with its template. So as a subcomponent via `$component: {$layers: "fluid.demos.imageGenerator"}`, the loader still applies and view is the SFC template. But with `fluid.selfTemplateViewComponent` semantics — "The markup its parent placed inside its @id element" — that's for when no template is supplied. The SFC template takes precedence (fluid.sfcTemplateViewComponent vs selfTemplate? whichever is in the merged view layers).

Hmm wait, there could be a conflict: the picture element for subcomponent views. For nested widgets inside an umbrella, conventionally you'd expect them to render into the umbrella's @id container with their own markup. The SFC template's root element: with `elideParent` default true, the template's root element merges with the container (@id element). That should be fine — the finder widget renders its padded box inside our container.

Alternatively, simpler and more robust: make the umbrella's own view, and give each subcomponent `view: "{umbrella}..."`? No — simplest: keep each subcomponent's own view; they render into the @id elements.

But hold on — is there an even simpler approach: a fresh SFC layer with `$layers: ["fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder", "fluid.demos.imageGenerator", "fluid.topLevelWidget"]`, merging both into ONE component, with a new combined template. The merged component would have both sets of members: closest, noteText, distance, distanceLine from finder; state, generate, etc. from generator. Its view: the SFC loader adds view with our new template. Parent layers contribute their view records — imageGenerator's? The imageGenerator's `view` — as said, not in its script; comes from the SFC loader when its SFC was loaded, i.e., a static `view` member added to the layer record `fluid.demos.imageGenerator`. When our layer lists it in $layers, the merge includes imageGenerator's `view` record (subcomponent precedence from that layer). But prepended - our own SFC-added view record has precedence... order: static layers linearised weakest first; the loader-added view for OUR layer is presumably at `subcomponent` precedence too, but ours is more derived → beats parents. However both records are `view: {$component: {...}}` — a key holding a `$component` signal record replaced as a unit by stronger value, not merged. Actually is `$component` a signal record? Yes. So our view (sfcTemplateViewComponent with our template) replaces imageGenerator's view (its SFC template). Good — no template conflict; the inherited view from finder has no template anyway (its view record is loader-added with the finder template!). Wait — fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder's stored layer definition includes the view added by its SFC loader (template "Closest Sticky Note"). When our layer inherits fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder, that view record comes along, but gets replaced by our own view record. Since $component records replace as a unit, we just get our template. 

But then the merged view template must include `@id` elements for the finder's `distanceLine` subcomponent and for... imageGenerator's subcomponents errorMessage, loadingMessage, result — OR we render everything inline in our combined template using the merged members (noteText, distance, state, etc.). Actually that's even better: one integrated UI — show closest note text, distance, a text input (maybe disabled or hidden?), a Generate button that uses the closest note as the prompt, generate/show-another buttons, and result.

The user: "move it close to a sticky note and press a button to generate an image". So core: prompt = closest note's text. What about the prompt text input? Keep it visible? The imageGenerator's prompt input writes state.prompt — if we leave input bound to state.prompt, the user can type, but the note's text would override? No — with merged layers, state.prompt is just the typed value; it doesn't automatically come from the note unless we wire it.

We could wire: `prompt: "$compute:..."`? Better: make the "Find Image" button use the closest note text. We could override `generate`? The `generate` method in imageGenerator takes `{self}.state` as argument and reads `state.prompt`. Hmm, `$method` args reference `{self}.state` — sited reference to the state root? Args reference `{self}.state` — resolved at call time as the reactive root value (sited). generate does `state.prompt.trim()` etc. We can't easily override just the prompt source... but we could override the `generate` method in our layer: `generate: {$method: {func: ..., args: ["{self}.state", "{self}.noteText"]}}` calling a variant that uses noteText as prompt.

Simplest variant: our layer defines its own `generate` method function that sets prompt from noteText then calls original? Since methods are signal records — our stronger layer replaces the generate record entirely. We can write:

```js
generate: {
    $method: {
        func: "fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator.generate",
        args: ["{self}.state", "{self}.noteText"]
    }
}
```

and define the JS function to call `fluid.demos.imageGenerator.generate` with a state whose prompt is the note text. But state is the reactive root object — generate mutates `state.isGenerating`, etc. Since the reference `{self}.state` is sited (mutable site), the mutations write through. But `state.prompt = noteText` mutation would also persist — fine.

Actually cleaner: our generate function:

```js
fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator.generate = function (state, prompt) {
    state.prompt = prompt;  // hmm, but sited write
    return fluid.demos.imageGenerator.generate(state);
};
```

Wait, but there's a subtlety: `{self}.state` as a method arg — is it sited? The docs: passing a reference as arg... "References anywhere within it are resolved." Is `{self}.state` resolved to the current value (a snapshot) or a sited reference? For $method args, a sited reference "writing through it writes to the original site". The original imageGenerator.generate does `state.prompt.trim(); state.isGenerating = true; state.images = images;` — and the live updates work in the real widget (it updates state). So `{self}.state` must be a sited reference into the reactive root. Per $reactiveRoot docs: "Writes at or beneath the root through the component proxy produce a fresh immutable value" — and references into it via valueAs etc. Yes, sited.

So our override works: setting `state.prompt = prompt` updates the live state, then calling the original generate uses it.

Hmm, but actually, is there a subtlety with async? generate returns a promise; fine.

Then the template: our combined template. We need `@onclick="{self}.generate()"` button. The original uses `@{buttonLabel}` and disabled `@{cannotGenerate}` — cannotGenerate checks `!prompt.trim()` — with prompt set at click time... wait, cannotGenerate is computed from state.prompt live; if state.prompt is empty until first click, the button would be disabled! Because cannotGenerate = isGenerating || !prompt.trim(). state.prompt starts "". So Generate button disabled initially. Need to override `cannotGenerate` in our layer to check noteText instead: `cannotGenerate: "$compute:fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator.cannotGenerate({self}.noteText, {self}.isGenerating)"` — or simply always enabled when there's a note. Similarly `errorMessage`/`loadingMessage` templates use @{...} of error/isGenerating — those still work since members inherited.

The input field: keep it? The user story: move near sticky note, press button, image from note text. Showing the input is optional. I'd keep the UI minimal but could hide the input and just show note text + generate button. But reusing the generator's result/error/loading subcomponents — their views are their own SFC templates (each has `view` in their layer records with templates). Wait no — in the merged-layer approach, the subcomponent records `errorMessage`, `loadingMessage`, `result` are in the merged component (from imageGenerator layer). Their `view`s are subcomponents of the merged component — they still have their own `$component.view` from the layer. Their templates render the error p, loading p, figure. Our umbrella template needs `@id` elements for them: `<div @id="errorMessage"></div>` etc. as the generator's template had.

And `distanceLine` from finder with its $if — also needs `@id="distanceLine"`.

Also `keyDown` method — generate on Enter; harmless to keep; our template's input would use it. If we drop the input entirely, keyDown unused — fine but let's keep an input bound to a display? Hmm.

Alternatively keep the prompt input in the template bound to state.prompt, and the Generate button uses the note; then after generation, prompt shows the note text. That seems nice: user sees what prompt was used. can also still type to override? But button ignores input — confusing. Maybe better keep it simple: label the button "Find Image" still, template with the input shown, but the note text displayed prominently (from finder widget). I think showing both is good UX: closest note text big, plus result area.

Let me now design the new SFC layer `fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator`:

```html
<script>
fluid.def("fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator", {
    $layers: ["fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder", "fluid.demos.imageGenerator", "fluid.topLevelWidget"],
    preferredSize: { w: 360, h: 560 },
    // Override the generate button to use the closest note's text as the prompt
    cannotGenerate: "$compute:fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator.cannotGenerate({self}.noteText, {self}.isGenerating)",
    generate: {
        $method: {
            func: "fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator.generate",
            args: ["{self}.state", "{self}.noteText"]
        }
    }
});

fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator.cannotGenerate = (noteText, isGenerating) =>
    isGenerating || !String(noteText).trim() || noteText === "No sticky notes found" || noteText === "Not placed on a canvas";
```

Hmm wait — noteText when no note: "Not placed on a canvas" or "No sticky notes found". The finder shows those messages in its text area. If we feed that as prompt, the image search would do something silly. Block generate in those cases. Actually maybe cleaner: only allow generation when distance !== null. `distance` is null when no note or not on canvas. So `cannotGenerate = isGenerating || {self}.distance === null`. That's simpler and ties to the "move it close to a sticky note" flow.

```js
fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator.cannotGenerate = (distance, isGenerating) =>
    isGenerating || distance === null;

fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator.generate = function (state, noteText) {
    state.prompt = noteText;
    return fluid.demos.imageGenerator.generate(state);
};
```

Careful: state.prompt write — the reference `{self}.state` sited → state.prompt = noteText writes through to live layer. Good.

preferredSize: finder prefers 260x200, generator 360x460. Combined view: finder's info compact (maybe drop the finder's h3 title? The finder's template is replaced by ours, so we control). Set preferredSize {w: 360, h: 500}.

Template design:

```html
<template>
    <div class="note-imagegen">
        <div class="note-imagegen-note">
            <div class="note-imagegen-note-text">@{{noteText}}</div>
            <div @id="distanceLine"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note-imagegen-controls">
            <button class="note-imagegen-button" disabled="@{cannotGenerate}" @onclick="{self}.generate()">Ask Pixabay</button>
            <button class="note-imagegen-button note-imagegen-another" disabled="@{cannotShowAnother}" @onclick="{self}.showAnother()">Another</button>
        </div>
        <div @id="errorMessage"></div>
        <div @id="loadingMessage"></div>
        <div @id="result"></div>
    </div>
</template>
```

Wait — @{{noteText}} full context syntax; `@{noteText}` shorthand works for `{self}.noteText` per docs. Use `@{noteText}`? The finder template uses @{{closestNoteFinder}.distance} with context nickname inside nested $if subcomponent (where self is the distanceLine component). In our template, self is the combined component, so `@{noteText}` and `@{distance}` suffice.

Styles: distinctive `note-imagegen-` classes.

Now, widget creation: the user selected both existing shapes; "Combine the functionality of the two selected widgets so I can move it close to a sticky note and press a button to generate an image". So create a NEW combined widget on the canvas. Should we delete the two old ones? "Combine the functionality of the two selected widgets" — minimal change: create the new combined widget. Replace the old ones? The two existing widgets could remain — but user likely wants a single combined widget to move around. I'd create the combined widget and delete the two originals? Hmm, "so I can move it close to a sticky note" — they want one widget. The two existing ones: finder already tracks closest note; generator separately. Creating combined and deleting the two selected seems right ("combine"). But deletion is destructive; undoing restore. The example response shows delete parts exist. I think the intent: merge them into one. I'll create the new widget (fresh id "shape:note-imagegen") positioned near their selection, and delete shape:finder and shape:imagegen.

Hmm, but maybe safer: create combined widget and keep the two originals? The user said "Combine the functionality of the two selected widgets so I can move it close to a sticky note" — a single merged widget replaces the two. I'll delete the two selected shapes and create the combined one, noting they can undo. Actually, deleting is risky if the user wanted to keep them for comparison. But "combine" strongly implies replacement. In tldraw canvas demos, the user arranges widgets; combining two selected into one = replace. I'll go with: new widget + delete both.

Where to place: selection at (100,100) 260x200 and (420,100) 360x460. Canvas notes at (120,420) and (960,140). Place combined at e.g. x=420, y=200? Put it near a note so user can immediately try: note-fox at (120,420). Place at (100, 640)? Canvas extents unknown; place near fox: e.g. x=140, y=660? Hmm, size 360x500 → (140, 660) might be offscreen? Canvas presumably large. Better: place beside selection at x=420? generator occupies y 100..560. Put at x=420, y=600? The fox note is at 120,420. Distance ~ 300px. Alternatively place between: x=350, y=420 → distance to fox 230, lighthouse far. I'll place at {"x": 360, "y": 380}? That overlaps generator (420..) no; x=360..360+360=720 overlaps shape:imagegen? No—imagegen occupies x 420–780, y 100–560. Overlap if y<560. Use x=360, y=620? Let me just place at x: 360, y: 620, size 360x520. Hmm actually simplest: near the fox note at (100, 700). I'll go x: 120, y: 700.

Hmm — wait. One important concern: does instantiating the merged layer require the SFC layers of both parents to be loaded, and does the merged `view` conflict? Also `preferredSize` — finder sets w260/h200; we override with w360/h520.

Another concern: `fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder` derives `closest` from `{tldrawHost}.notes` and `{self}.hostBounds`. hostBounds is provided by fluid.topLevelWidget from tldraw view. As the merged component is a topLevelWidget itself, fine — the finder part works because its members are on the merged component itself.

Also the merged layer's view: we define as SFC, so loader adds `view` with fluid.sfcTemplateViewComponent and our template. Parent layers' view records (finder SFC's view, generator SFC's view) — do they exist in the stored layer records? The SFC loader patches the script before execution: adds view subcomponent. So the layer record `fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder` includes `view: {$component: {$layers: "fluid.sfcTemplateViewComponent", template: ...}}`. When our layer inherits and merges, view records at subcomponent precedence merge... "A key holding a signal record is replaced as a unit" — view is a $component record in each parent. The strongest wins wholesale — our SFC loader-added view (added when OUR SFC script executes — after the layer is defined). Hmm, when is the loader's view-added applied relative to inheritance? The base layer record gets the view added at load; that's part of the stored layer. Merger: our stored record's view replaces both parents' views entirely. Good.

But wait — there's a subtlety: is `view` in parent layers `{$component: {...}}` — the $component records merge among themselves? "Plain objects contributed by more than one layer merge recursively, key by key. Any other value (signal record) from a stronger layer replaces that from a weaker wholesale." So parent1.view.$component (object) and parent2.view.$component and ours — wait `view` itself is the key holding `{$component:...}` — a signal record at key `view`. Stronger replaces at key `view` as a unit? But precedence: def (static layers) — closestNoteFinder's view from its file is part of its layer record (def precedence); imageGenerator's view also def; ours is also def (loader-added at same time our layer recorded). C3 linearisation: our layer beats both parents; earlier parents beat later. But are two $component records at the same precedence merged? The rule says only plain objects merge recursively; a key holding a signal record is replaced as a unit by a stronger layer — so ours replaces both. But then how did "A view inherited from another layer accumulates the configuration" work? Because there, configuration keys like `elideParent: false` sit *inside* the view.$component plain object contributed by different layers — view.$component bodies merge recursively as plain objects, including their `$layers`? Hmm, `$layers` is a string vs array... The docs example: `fluid.def("my.footer", {view: {$component: {elideParent: false}}})` accumulates with inherited view config. So view.$component plain-object bodies from different layers DO merge key-by-key (each is a plain object contribution at its layer). But if a layer supplies an entire `template` string and another supplies another — same key, stronger replaces key. And if one layer supplies `view: "{other}.view"`... whatever. For us: parents contribute view.$component objects containing $layers+template (from SFC loader) — they'd merge into one object with two different `template` keys?? No — same key `template` from both: linearised order, later beats earlier → one template wins (whichever parent is more derived; tie → earlier-listed in $layers wins). Then ours adds its own `template` — our layer record's view.$component.template replaces. Since ours is the most derived, our template wins. Also `$layers` keys: finder view $layers "fluid.sfcTemplateViewComponent" and generator's same → fine.

But hmm — the `template` strings: our template replaces. However careful: parent view.$component also might carry `shapeId`? No, shapeId is passed via potentia view.$component {shapeId: "shape:..."} — that's potentia/user precedence, merges into view.$component, fine.

Also `fluid.topLevelWidget` parent — in $layers last. Both finder and generator already have it. fine.

Another subtlety: `distanceLine`'s $if uses `func: distance => distance !== null, args: "{closestNoteFinder}.distance"` — context `closestNoteFinder` (nickname of layer fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder). In the merged component, the component has layer fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder (inherited) — nicknames: its own names include member name and layer names — the component itself is named `closestNoteFinder`? The context search: "A component bearing that layer, in scope" — the component itself bears that layer (its layers include fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder) and scope includes self's own names: "its own names" — names are member name + non-framework layer names + nicknames. Wait — names of the component include its layer names? "The names of a component are its member name and each of its non-framework layer names and their nicknames." So the merged component IS in scope as `closestNoteFinder` (nickname) and `imageGenerator`. So `{closestNoteFinder}.distance` resolves: locate component named closestNoteFinder in scope — the merged component itself. Good, still works.

Similarly `{imageGenerator}.error` inside errorMessage's template — the errorMessage subcomponent's view template `@{{imageGenerator}.error}` — scope search from errorMessage: up to merged component, which has nickname imageGenerator. Works.

Now `generate` override: my layer defines `generate: {$method: {func: fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator.generate, args: [...]} }` at def precedence, strongest among static. Replaces generator's. Good.

`cannotGenerate` override similarly.

But wait — will `buttonLabel` ("Find..." / "Finding...") still work? Yes inherit. The user said "press a button to generate an image" — keep label "Ask Pixabay"? Keep default "Find Image" maybe clearer with note context. I'll hardcode "Generate Image" in template? Using @{buttonLabel} keeps states. Let me label buttons: Generate button text `@{{Generate an image}}`? Just use literal text "Generate Image from Note" plus loading state? Simpler: static label "Generate Image". Then is the button disabled during generating? cannotGenerate covers isGenerating. I'll use a static label to keep it explicit, but inherit `cannotShowAnother` for the Another button.

Hmm, wait — one more check on overriding `generate` while `keyDown` (Enter) uses `{self}.increment`? No — keyDown: `e.key === "Enter" && !self.isGenerating ? self.generate() : undefined` — calls self.generate() with no args → our generate method with args ["{self}.state", "{self}.noteText"] — positional args unused. Fine. We may omit the input from template, so keyDown unused.

Also `refresh` on image error uses `{self}.state` fine.

Template: do we include the text input? If we omit the input, fine. I'll show the note text and a prompt echo maybe. Keep it clean:

```html
<template>
    <div class="note-imagegen">
        <div class="note-imagegen-banner">Image from the closest sticky note</div>
        <div class="note-imagegen-note">
            <span class="note-imagegen-note-label">Closest note:</span>
            <span class="note-imagegen-note-text">@{noteText}</span>
        </div>
        <div @id="distanceLine"></div>
        <div class="note-imagegen-prompt">Prompt: "@{state.prompt}"</div>
        <div class="note-imagegen-buttons">
            <button class="note-imagegen-button" disabled="@{cannotGenerate}" @onclick="{self}.generate()">Generate Image</button>
            <button class="note-imagegen-button note-imagegen-another" disabled="@{cannotShowAnother}" @onclick="{self}.showAnother()">Another</button>
        </div>
        <div @id="errorMessage"></div>
        <div @id="loadingMessage"></div>
        <div @id="result"></div>
    </div>
</template>
```

Prompt echo shows note text after generation — nice feedback. state.prompt inherited.

Styles for note-imagegen classes. Use flex column.

Shape placement: I'll put at {"x": 120, "y": 660, "w": 360, "h": 520}. Actually h can be omitted (preferredSize). I'll include w/h in preferredSize and give only x,y in shape part. simpler: shape {"x": 100, "y": 680}.

Hmm wait, is canvas likely y-limited? Unknown; notes at y420 h200 → to 620. Placing at 680 leaves room-ish. Canvas sizes in these demos are like 1400x1000. OK.

